---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import PageCTA from '../components/PageCTA.astro';
import TermSwap from '../components/TermSwap.astro';

const title = 'Memory · metalmind';
const description = 'Save once, recall in 45ms median, zero standing tool-schema tax. Plain markdown in your own folder - Obsidian-compatible, not required.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <PageHero
    metal="copper"
    eyebrow="recall · save"
    eyebrowScadrial="tap copper · store copper"
    title="The headline. The thing the rest of the library compounds back into."
    sub="Save once. Recall in 45 ms median. Zero standing tool-schema tax. Your notes stay plain markdown in your own folder (Obsidian-compatible - open them in any editor, in any tool, on any device) - Claude reads them verbatim, not as LLM-extracted summaries."
  />

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="feat-grid">
        <div class="feat-card">
          <div class="feat-num mono accent-text">01</div>
          <h3 class="display feat-h">Save once</h3>
          <p>
            <TermSwap classic="save" scadrial="store copper" mono />
            deposits a decision into your local markdown vault (Obsidian-compatible). metalmind proposes the path, wikilinks, and frontmatter; you approve; it writes.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="feat-card">
          <div class="feat-num mono accent-text">02</div>
          <h3 class="display feat-h">Recall, no MCP tax</h3>
          <p>
            <TermSwap classic="recall" scadrial="tap copper" mono />
            is a Bash call, not an MCP tool. Zero schema bloat per session. A loopback HTTP server inside the watcher handles calls in sub-100 ms; stdio MCP is the always-on fallback.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="feat-card">
          <div class="feat-num mono accent-text">03</div>
          <h3 class="display feat-h">Session-start awareness</h3>
          <p>A SessionStart hook plus a stamped block in <span class="kbd">~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</span> teach Claude when to recall - no "did you check memory?" prompting, ever.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="feat-card">
          <div class="feat-num mono accent-text">04</div>
          <h3 class="display feat-h">Vault writes without drift</h3>
          <p>
            <TermSwap classic="note" scadrial="scribe" mono />
            is the CRUD interface agents use instead of raw Write. Nine verbs - create, update, patch, supersede, delete, archive, rename, list, show. It stamps frontmatter, picks the right folder, auto-links the project MOC, and rewrites <span class="kbd">[[wikilinks]]</span> across the vault on rename.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="feat-card">
          <div class="feat-num mono accent-text">05</div>
          <h3 class="display feat-h">Decisions that supersede, not vanish</h3>
          <p>
            <TermSwap classic="note supersede" scadrial="scribe supersede" mono />
            <span class="kbd">{`<old> <new>`}</span> marks a decision replaced by its successor. Recall drops the old note to 0.4x and every hit from it carries <span class="kbd">superseded_by</span>, so an agent lands on current truth without the history being deleted.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="feat-card">
          <div class="feat-num mono accent-text">06</div>
          <h3 class="display feat-h">Claims checked against the code</h3>
          <p>
            A note can carry <span class="kbd">code: ["repo#symbol"]</span> refs. <span class="kbd">--verify-code</span> and <span class="kbd">doctor</span> flag refs whose symbol no longer exists in your forge-registered repos - the decision outlives the code, but you find out when it does.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="feat-card">
          <div class="feat-num mono accent-text">07</div>
          <h3 class="display feat-h">Native memory as a feeder</h3>
          <p>
            <span class="kbd">metalmind ingest auto-memory</span> imports Claude Code's own auto-memory topic files into the vault as <span class="kbd">Memory/</span> notes - hash-guarded and idempotent, and it reports a conflict rather than clobbering a note you edited.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="feat-card">
          <div class="feat-num mono accent-text">08</div>
          <h3 class="display feat-h">Fusion that reads the query</h3>
          <p>
            Exact-match tokens (UUIDs, ticket IDs, hostnames) raise the keyword leg automatically. Fused scores are folder-weighted - <span class="kbd">Archive/</span> 0.4x, <span class="kbd">Inbox/</span> 0.7x - so stale notes re-rank below in-flight work instead of being excluded.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div class="eyebrow">§ Recall benchmarks</div>
        <h2>Quality holds at scale.</h2>
        <p>Hybrid retrieval (semantic + BM25 fused via RRF, with optional cross-encoder rerank). Measured on hand-authored gold notes plus same-domain distractors.</p>
      </div>
      <table class="proof-table mono memory-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>vault size</th><th>hybrid hit@1</th><th>hybrid hit@5</th><th>+rerank hit@1</th><th>+rerank hit@5</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>12 notes</td><td>90%</td><td>95%</td><td class="accent-text">90%</td><td>95%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>100 notes</td><td>85%</td><td>95%</td><td class="accent-text">90%</td><td>95%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>500 notes</td><td>85%</td><td>90%</td><td class="accent-text">90%</td><td>95%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1,000 notes</td><td>85%</td><td>85%</td><td class="accent-text">90%</td><td>95%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <PageCTA heading="Claude never meets you cold again." />
</BaseLayout>
